{"uid":"828537010449dbdf","name":"test_interrupt_targets_the_pid_directly_before_the_child_has_its_own_session","fullName":"tests.test_startup_watchdog_cpu_progress#test_interrupt_targets_the_pid_directly_before_the_child_has_its_own_session","historyId":"a4845712e4f52cbf924a8bccb168e481","time":{"start":1791867565715,"stop":1791867565715,"duration":0},"description":"Task 001: the pre-`setsid` window that caused the gate red at\n    `472d055` (`test_a_child_that_consumes_no_cpu_is_still_killed_inside_its_\n    window` there recorded duration_s=61.06, exit_code=-9, timed_out=True).\n\n    `start_new_session=True` makes the child call `os.setsid()` before it\n    execs `pi`, but nothing orders that call against this method's read of\n    `os.getpgid(child_pid)`. In the window before `setsid()` has run, the\n    child's process group is still the ENGINE's own group (inherited from\n    the fork) -- so the old `os.killpg(os.getpgid(pid), SIGINT)` would\n    signal the engine's own group, not the child, and the SIGINT never\n    reaches it. This drives exactly that window with no real timing race:\n    `os.getpgid` is made to report the engine's own pgid for the child pid,\n    the shape a genuinely pre-`setsid` child has.\n\n    Mutation case (recorded in the commit message): reverting `interrupt()`\n    to the unconditional `os.killpg(os.getpgid(pid), SIGINT)` makes this\n    test fail -- `killpg` gets called with the engine's own pgid instead of\n    `kill` being called on the child's pid, and the assertions below catch\n    that directly rather than through a timing-dependent kill outcome.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Task 001: the pre-<code>setsid</code> window that caused the gate red at<br />\n<code>472d055</code> (<code>test_a_child_that_consumes_no_cpu_is_still_killed_inside_its_ window</code> there recorded duration_s=61.06, exit_code=-9, timed_out=True).</p>\n<pre><code>`start_new_session=True` makes the child call `os.setsid()` before it\nexecs `pi`, but nothing orders that call against this method's read of\n`os.getpgid(child_pid)`. In the window before `setsid()` has run, the\nchild's process group is still the ENGINE's own group (inherited from\nthe fork) -- so the old `os.killpg(os.getpgid(pid), SIGINT)` would\nsignal the engine's own group, not the child, and the SIGINT never\nreaches it. This drives exactly that window with no real timing race:\n`os.getpgid` is made to report the engine's own pgid for the child pid,\nthe shape a genuinely pre-`setsid` child has.\n\nMutation case (recorded in the commit message): reverting `interrupt()`\nto the unconditional `os.killpg(os.getpgid(pid), SIGINT)` makes this\ntest fail -- `killpg` gets called with the engine's own pgid instead of\n`kill` being called on the child's pid, and the assertions below catch\nthat directly rather than through a timing-dependent kill outcome.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791866910604,"stop":1791866910605,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791866910605,"stop":1791866910605,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791867565691,"stop":1791867565691,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_isolate_ambient_home_in_process","time":{"start":1791867565692,"stop":1791867565699,"duration":7},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shou